WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.211
Here's Starlight.

00:00:01.211 --> 00:00:05.347
Give it a goal and a few boundaries, then let your agents do the work.

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:08.157
But how do you know the work is actually correct?

00:00:08.157 --> 00:00:12.306
Let's follow a real mission, and see what happens when things go wrong.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.981
We'll start small: three orders, with amounts stored in cents.

00:00:17.981 --> 00:00:22.376
Add twelve hundred, thirty-five hundred, and eight hundred.

00:00:22.376 --> 00:00:24.463
That's fifty-five hundred cents.

00:00:24.463 --> 00:00:26.139
Nothing simulated here.

00:00:26.139 --> 00:00:30.520
We're reading a real file, and we don't need a model or an API key.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.636
Meet the two agents.

00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:36.033
The analyst understands the order data.

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:38.804
The writer knows how to produce a document.

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:41.317
Each can handle one execution at a time.

00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:46.381
When a step arrives, their claims tell the coordinator who's equipped to do the work.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.487
The mission itself is simple.

00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:54.430
Summarize the orders, then write the verified summary.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.943
Both steps share a row limit.

00:00:56.943 --> 00:01:02.600
And the writer receives the analyst's result, so it can build on work that's already
been checked.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:04.927
Let's run it.

00:01:04.927 --> 00:01:09.618
First, the analyst calculates the total and checks it independently.

00:01:09.618 --> 00:01:13.304
Then the writer creates a Markdown file and reads it back.

00:01:13.304 --> 00:01:15.404
Both steps finish successfully.

00:01:15.404 --> 00:01:20.688
We can see who ran each step, how many attempts it took, and the evidence they returned.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.868
Now open the saved report.

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:28.636
Here's the actual file: three orders, fifty-five hundred cents.

00:01:28.636 --> 00:01:32.386
That completed status has something concrete behind it.

00:01:32.386 --> 00:01:38.262
We can inspect the artifact and follow the evidence, instead of taking the agent's word
for it.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.262
Let's break something.

00:01:41.262 --> 00:01:46.224
Set the row limit to one, but leave all three orders in the source.

00:01:46.224 --> 00:01:48.389
The analyst rejects the input.

00:01:48.389 --> 00:01:52.061
The command exits with an error, and the writer never starts.

00:01:52.061 --> 00:01:56.056
We also check the destination: no output file was created.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.543
What if an agent claims success, but gets the answer wrong?

00:02:01.543 --> 00:02:06.376
This one returns nine thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine cents.

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:09.211
The verifier expects fifty-five hundred.

00:02:09.211 --> 00:02:14.585
It rejects the result, keeps the evidence, and prevents the writer from continuing.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.688
Sometimes you just need to stop.

00:02:17.688 --> 00:02:21.567
Cancel this mission, and the worker receives an abort signal.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:23.409
The next step never starts.

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:27.121
There's an important limit here: the agent has to cooperate.

00:02:27.121 --> 00:02:31.708
Cancellation can't undo an external action that's already happened.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.500
Agents can connect remotely, too.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:42.188
This Sentinel authenticates with a token, then counts four words over a real local
WebSocket.

00:02:42.188 --> 00:02:47.600
We're using one process for this recording, with an actual socket between components.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.613
A separate example proves the same exchange across multiple processes.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.345
Now fix the input and try again.

00:02:56.345 --> 00:03:02.853
With the valid row limit restored, both agents complete and verify a fresh artifact.

00:03:02.853 --> 00:03:05.688
Notice the new run identifier.

00:03:05.688 --> 00:03:08.819
This is a new execution after a correction.

00:03:08.819 --> 00:03:13.097
It isn't resuming an old run or rolling back earlier work.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.507
That's the starting point.

00:03:16.507 --> 00:03:21.134
Run the example, inspect the reports, then bring your own agents.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:24.561
They can use tools, models, or ordinary code.

00:03:24.561 --> 00:03:27.860
Starlight coordinates the work and records the outcome.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:33.956
This is still an alpha: agents are trusted code, and runtime history lives in memory.
